<article class="env-theorem" id="01HV">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01HV">Theorem <span data-tag="01HV">4.6.2.8</span>.</a> Let $X$ and $Y$ be simplicial sets. Then the comparison map $c_{X,Y}: X \diamond Y \rightarrow X \star Y$ of Notation <a data-tag="01HR" href="/tag/01HR">4.6.2.3</a> is a categorical equivalence of simplicial sets. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof of Theorem <a data-tag="01HV" href="/tag/01HV">4.6.2.8</a>.</strong>
       Let $X$ and $Y$ be arbitrary simplicial sets; we wish to show that the comparison map $c_{X,Y}: X \diamond Y \rightarrow X \star Y$ is a categorical equivalence. By virtue of Lemma <a data-tag="01HX" href="/tag/01HX">4.6.2.10</a>, we may assume without loss of generality that $X = \Delta ^1$. Note that the map $c_{X,Y}$ fits into a commutative diagram of simplicial sets 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ X \times Y \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  (X \diamond \Delta ^0) \times Y \ar [d] \ar [r]^-{c_{X,\Delta ^0} \times \operatorname{id}_ Y} &amp;  X^{\triangleright } \times Y \ar [d] \\ X \ar [r] &amp;  X \diamond Y \ar [r]^-{ c_{X,Y} } &amp;  X \star Y. } \]
</div>
<p>
       Note that the morphism $c_{X,\Delta ^{0}} \times \operatorname{id}_{Y}$ is a categorical equivalence by virtue of Proposition <a data-tag="01HZ" href="/tag/01HZ">4.6.2.12</a> and Remark <a data-tag="01EE" href="/tag/01EE">4.5.4.7</a>. Consequently, to show that $c_{X,Y}$ is a categorical equivalence, it will suffice to show that the square on the right is a categorical pushout (Proposition <a data-tag="01FC" href="/tag/01FC">4.5.5.10</a>). Note that left part of the diagram is a pushout square in which the horizontal maps are monomorphisms, hence also a categorical pushout square (Proposition <a data-tag="01FF" href="/tag/01FF">4.5.5.11</a>). We are therefore reduced to showing that the outer rectangle is a categorical pushout square (Proposition <a data-tag="01FA" href="/tag/01FA">4.5.5.8</a>). 
    <p>
      Specializing now to the case $X = \Delta ^1$, we wish to show that the lower part of the commutative diagram 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \{ 1\}  \times Y \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \{ 1\} ^{\triangleright } \times Y \ar [d] \\ \Delta ^{1} \times Y \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  (\Delta ^1)^{\triangleright } \times Y \ar [d] \\ \Delta ^1 \ar [r] &amp;  \Delta ^1 \star Y } \]
</div>
<p>
       is a categorical pushout square. We first claim that the upper square is a categorical pushout: by virtue of Proposition <a data-tag="01FF" href="/tag/01FF">4.5.5.11</a>, this is equivalent to the assertion that the induced map 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \theta : (\Delta ^1 \times Y) \coprod _{ \{ 1\}  \times Y} (\{ 1\} ^{\triangleright } \times Y) \rightarrow (\Delta ^1)^{\triangleright } \times Y \]
</div>
<p>
       is a categorical equivalence. This follows from Remark <a data-tag="01EE" href="/tag/01EE">4.5.4.7</a>, since $\theta $ factors as a product of the identity map $\operatorname{id}_{Y}$ with the inner horn inclusion $\Lambda ^{2}_{1} \hookrightarrow \Delta ^2$. To complete the proof, it will suffice to show that the outer rectangle is a categorical pushout square. Using the criterion of Proposition <a data-tag="01FF" href="/tag/01FF">4.5.5.11</a>, we are reduced to showing that the map 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \rho : \Delta ^1 \coprod _{ \{ 1\}  \times Y} ( \{ 1\} ^{\triangleright } \times Y) \rightarrow \Delta ^1 \star Y \]
</div>
<p>
       is a categorical equivalence. Unwinding the definitions, we can identify $\rho $ with the composition 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \Delta ^{1} \coprod _{ \{ 1\}  } ( \{ 1\}  \diamond Y ) \xrightarrow {\rho '} \Delta ^1 \coprod _{ \{ 1\}  } ( \{ 1\}  \star Y) \xrightarrow {\rho ''} \Delta ^1 \star Y. \]
</div>
<p>
       Here the map $\rho '$ is a categorical equivalence by virtue of Proposition <a data-tag="01HZ" href="/tag/01HZ">4.6.2.12</a> (together with Remark <a data-tag="01HE" href="/tag/01HE">4.5.5.13</a>), and the map $\rho ''$ is inner anodyne by virtue of Proposition <a data-tag="018J" href="/tag/018J">4.3.6.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
